## 1. Third-party quiz content — removed

An earlier draft of this repository included a quiz derived from **PCEP-30-02
Topic 8, pages 407-434**, part of the *Certified Entry-Level Python Programmer*
exam-preparation material published by the **Python Institute / OpenEDG**, a
commercial vendor. 28 of its 30 items were adapted from that work.

Those items have been **removed** and the file deleted. Adapting a commercial
exam product does not convey the right to redistribute it, and publishing the
result under this project's MIT licence would have asserted a licence the
project does not hold. Nothing derived from that work remains in the repository.

The two original items from that file — on list equality versus identity, and
on tuple indexing — were kept, because they test generic Python semantics rather
than anything specific to the source. They now live in
`quizzes/core_equality_and_indexing.json`, are marked original, and no longer
carry the `pcep-` identifier or the "PCEP-aligned" label, which would have
implied a derivation that no longer exists.

PCEP and PCEP-30-02 are trademarks of OpenEDG. This project is not affiliated
with or endorsed by the Python Institute, and the source work is not
redistributed here.

## 2. Vendored third-party code

`static/vendor/cm/` contains a vendored build of **CodeMirror 6**, together with
its Lezer parser packages (`@lezer/python`, `@lezer/common`, `@lezer/lr`,
`@lezer/highlight`) and the `style-mod` and `w3c-keyname` dependencies. These are
unmodified third-party ES modules, redistributed as source.

Their licence terms are the upstream ones and they are **not** covered by this
project's MIT licence. Each vendored file ships next to the bundle it covers as
`<bundle>.LICENSE`, carrying the original copyright and permission notice —
`state.LICENSE`, `view.LICENSE`, `python.LICENSE` and the rest. Those files are
the authoritative terms, and they are MIT-licensed, copyright Marijn Haverbeke
and others. Nothing in this repository modifies them.

The bundles are served directly to the browser through an import map
(`static/vendor/cm/importmap.json`); there is no npm dependency, no bundler and
no build step for the editor.

